\f1\fs22 Eloise's office beneath Ruined Castle Gully had the kind of view that estate agents described as "intimate" and normal people called "a concrete wall." The lack of windows was apparently a feature, not a bug\'97something about avoiding distractions while plotting world domination through kitchen appliances.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah shifted in her chair, trying to find a position where Little Clinton wasn't using her organs as percussion instruments. Across from her, The Johnny had arranged himself like a magazine cover, all angles and unearned confidence. He'd been back on the payroll for exactly four days and had already submitted three expense reports for "research stimulants" that were definitely just champagne.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"We need three more discs," Eloise announced, her fingers steepled in what she probably practiced in mirrors. "Johnny's calculations suggest five in pentagonal formation will achieve stable resonance."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"My calculations are poetry in motion," The Johnny added, buffing his nails on his lab coat. "I've essentially revolutionized our understanding of reality. You're welcome."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah resisted the urge to remind him that his last revolution had resulted in what the insurance companies were calling "an act of Johnny." Instead, she focused on not vomiting. Morning sickness had evolved into all-day nausea with delusions of grandeur.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"The question," Eloise continued, "is procurement. Thanks to Hayley's adventurous breaking and entering, we know every stasis unit contains a Gyges Disc. Every refrigerator, freezer, and wine cooler in the country is secretly powered by reality-warping technology."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Which Fridge Force has hidden since the technology was invented," Sarah said. "Because apparently keeping beer cold is more important than understanding the fundamental nature of existence."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Profitable," Eloise corrected. "The word you're looking for is profitable. Do you know what the stasis industry is worth? More than the GDP of several small nations. Nations I could buy, incidentally, but choose not to. The paperwork is tedious."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Johnny leaned forward with the expression of someone about to contribute nothing of value. "So we simply take them. I know people who know people who know burglars."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"From where?" Sarah asked, though she already dreaded the answer.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise produced a tablet showing property listings that looked like wealth having a midlife crisis. "Mountain mansions. The Airborne own hundreds of vacation homes they visit perhaps once a year. Sometimes never. They just sit there, empty monuments to tax avoidance."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"With security systems," Sarah pointed out.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Oh yes. Extensive ones. Motion sensors, laser grids, automated defense turrets that shoot anything poorer than a certain net worth." Eloise smiled. "But the stasis units themselves? Standard models. The rich buy the same refrigerators as everyone else, they just fill them with more expensive disappointments."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"So we what, break into abandoned mansions and steal their kitchen appliances?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Precisely. Though 'we' is doing heavy lifting here." Eloise's smile could have curdled milk, assuming it wasn't in a stasis unit. "My organization could handle it, naturally. But I prefer to maintain distance from anything that might antagonize Fridge Force. They're so touchy about their monopoly. All those indefinite detentions off-shore. Very bad for the complexion, I'm told. All that salt air and despair."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah's stomach performed a maneuver that would have impressed gymnasts. "So hire professionals."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Or," Eloise said, her voice taking on that special quality reserved for suggestions that ended civilizations, "we use the Elvi."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Absolutely not."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"They're surprisingly competent. And you are their Holy Momma. One word from you\'97"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"No." Sarah's hand moved to her stomach where Little Clinton was apparently attempting to spell out protest slogans in kicks. "The Elvi are already fighting each other over gospel music. Half of them think the other half are heretics. They're in no condition to\'97"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"To what? Liberate some discs from empty houses owned by people who probably forgot they existed?" The Johnny examined his reflection in a spoon. "Sounds like exactly the kind of righteous mission they'd embrace."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"You don't get to have an opinion," Sarah snapped. "You've been back for less than a week."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Four days, actually. But who's counting?" He smiled. "Besides me. I'm counting. Do you know they make me clock in now? Me! I have to use a time card like some kind of... person who uses time cards."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise sighed, the sound of patience committing suicide. "Sarah, darling. Let me explain how this works. We need those discs. The project needs those discs. The prophecy that's growing inside you like the world's most consequential parasite needs those discs."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Then find another way."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"There is no other way. Unless you'd prefer I send formal requisitions to Fridge Force? 'Dear fascist refrigeration overlords, please give us your reality-warping technology so we can birth a new universe. Sincerely, your local weapons manufacturer.'" She paused. "Actually, that might work. They're remarkably literal sometimes."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah stood, which took more effort than it should have. Gravity and pregnancy were not friends. "I won't let you use the Elvi as expendable assets."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Expendable? I prefer 'creatively deployed.'" Eloise touched her tablet, and the wall screen lit up with security footage. "Do you remember what happened last time you defied me? That poor man from accounting. Gerald something. He made such interesting noises."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Gerald Henderson. And he survived."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Did he? Survival is such a broad term. He's alive, yes. But he flinches every time someone says 'dental plan.'" More footage appeared. "I have forty-two international dentist assassins at my disposal. They're in the building right now, actually. Team building exercise. They're making friendship bracelets out of floss. Very touching."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Johnny perked up. "Are they single?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Focus," both women said simultaneously.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah gripped the edge of the desk. "You can threaten me all you want. Send your entire oral hygiene death squad. I don't care. The Elvi have been through enough. I won't add breaking and entering to their troubles."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Such maternal instinct. How appropriate, given your condition." Eloise's voice could have flash-frozen hope. "Very well. We'll table this discussion. But Sarah? Tables are temporary. My patience less so."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah headed for the door, each step a small victory against architectural intimidation. "We'll find another way."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Of course we will. Johnny, see her out. Make sure she doesn't vomit on anything expensive. Which is everything."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Johnny scrambled after her, his cologne announcing his presence like an olfactory air raid. "You know, if you need someone to liaise with the Elvi\'97"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Johnny," Sarah said without turning, "if you go within fifty feet of the Ghetto, I'll personally ensure your next experiment involves discovering what happens when you put a Gyges Disc where the sun doesn't shine."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"That's... actually not a bad hypothesis. The human body is essentially a stasis unit for consciousness. If we could\'97"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah slammed the door on his theoretical proctology. Behind her, through the walls, she could hear Eloise making calls. Plans within plans, all of them probably involving someone's teeth.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Three more discs. That's all they needed. Three more pieces of reality-warping technology hiding in rich people's unused kitchens.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah touched her stomach, where Little Clinton performed what felt like an interpretive dance about property rights.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Don't worry," she whispered. "Mommy's not going to let the nice lady turn your uncles into cat burglars. We'll find another way."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The baby kicked in what she chose to interpret as skepticism.\

